#:layer #!pragmatics

^["intent"]{
 ~#goal: "Extend the marker system from a preserved taxonomy into an extraction, seed, and semantic contract surface. Use the current `family:qualifier` chain as the coarse selector, then design a lightweight metadata layer that helps later system decomposition without making comments noisy."
 ~#taste: "clarity, correctness, expressiveness"
}

^["stream"]{
 >> [2026-03-05 19:50] observe — the clean post-coverage audit reports 122 markers, but the dominant families remain flat (`todo`, `boundary`, `term`) even after the structured rewrite shipped.
 >> [2026-03-05 19:50] observe — the most useful structured markers today are implementation-adjacent: `portable:seed` (12), `portable:runtime` (3), `boundary:reset` (4), `types:debt` (2), `async:debt` (1).
 >> [2026-03-05 19:50] decide — the next phase should make markers useful for feature execution and later extraction, not only for audit inventory.
 >> [2026-03-05 19:50] decide — keep the chain syntax as the stable outer shell and evaluate an optional attribute bag for machine-actionable fields.
 >> [2026-03-05 19:50] taste — the marker surface should stay readable enough to survive hand-authored comments in TS, Rust, Python, Markdown, and `.spw`.
 >> [2026-03-06 03:02] ship — the contract bag, extraction/seed examples, pilot retags, and craft-skill guidance landed on `main@07ffc5a`.
 >> [2026-03-06 03:02] decide — treat `system`, `extract`, `semantic`, `basis`, `next`, and `density` as the stable first-wave keys; reserve `target` for real future ports only.
}

^["open"]{
 ?["should the doc path and plan slug be renamed from portability to extraction-seed to match the shipped semantics?"]
 ?["which next corpus surfaces deserve starter/kernel/scaffold markers after the pilot runtime and seed files?"]
}

